\section{Base R charts}{

A Base R chart is read from the plotting calls recorded on the current
device, and is the page that device shows: the last one. R draws a
high-level plot on a new page when it moves past the last panel of the
page it is on -- every plot on a page of one panel, the fifth under
\code{par(mfrow = c(2, 2))}, the first after \code{par(mfrow = )}, \code{par(mfcol = )}
or \code{layout()} sets a page up again -- and \code{plot.new()} and \code{frame()}
move on a panel as a plot does. So after \verb{hist(a); hist(b)} the chart
is the histogram of \code{b} alone, and after five plots under
\code{par(mfrow = c(2, 2))} it is the fifth, in the first panel of a 2 x 2
grid. Each plot of a grid is in the panel R drew it in: a plot drawn
after \code{par(new = TRUE)}, or with \code{add = TRUE}, is drawn in the panel of
the plot before it, one \code{par(mfg = )} sends to a panel out of turn is in
that panel, and a panel \code{plot.new()} or \code{frame()} passed over stays
empty. So it is where the call that set the grid up was not recorded:
one made through \code{graphics::par()}, before \code{\link[=maidr_on]{maidr_on()}}, or before an
earlier \code{show()} or \code{save_html()} on the device. A plot drawn in a region of the page \code{par(fig = )} gave it,
as an inset is, or in a screen of \code{split.screen()}, is drawn in that
region, and read with the plot before it. \code{lines()}, \code{points()},
\code{abline()}, \code{text()}, \code{legend()},
\code{title()}, \code{axis()} and the other low-level calls add to the plot they
are drawn on. Drawn on a panel \code{plot.new()} or \code{frame()} took, as a
legend of its own is, or on a plot maidr does not record, such as
\code{smoothScatter()}, they are drawn there, and read as part of no plot.
Nothing drawn on an
earlier page reaches the chart: not its data, its titles or the size it
is drawn at. A page \code{replayPlot()} puts back, from a plot
\code{recordPlot()} saved on a device that keeps a display list, is the page
the chart is read from, with what was drawn on it when it was saved and
what has been drawn on it since. A page that holds no plot maidr recorded -- one R started
with \code{plot.new()} or \code{frame()}, with a plot maidr does not record, or
with one drawn while \code{\link[=maidr_off]{maidr_off()}} was in effect, even with \code{lines()} or
\code{text()} drawn on it since -- is not read as the plot before it: \code{show()}
and \code{save_html()} stop, and say so. Each figure
of an R Markdown or Quarto document is read the same way, from the calls
on its own page.
}

\section{Chart size}{

A chart is drawn at a size in inches, as \code{\link[ggplot2:ggsave]{ggplot2::ggsave()}} and knitr's
\code{fig.width} and \code{fig.height} size a figure, and its SVG is 72 pixels to
the inch: a 10 x 4 in chart is 720 pixels wide and 288 high. The size is
the room the chart is laid out in -- how far apart its ticks and labels
are, how lattice arranges its panels, where Base R puts its titles -- and
not what maidr reads out: the data, titles and axis labels a reader
hears are the same at every size. Only how a reader moves between panels
can change: lattice arranges the panels of a chart conditioned on one
variable with no \verb{layout =} for the shape of the page, 1 x 2 at 7 x 5 in
and 2 x 1 at 5 x 8 in for two panels, and the subplot grid a reader
moves through follows; give \verb{layout =} to keep it. The size is set by
\code{width} and \code{height} in \code{\link[=show]{show()}} and \code{\link[=save_html]{save_html()}}, by \code{fig_width} and
\code{fig_height} in
\code{\link[=render_maidr]{render_maidr()}}, and by the chunk's \code{fig.width} and \code{fig.height} in an
R Markdown or Quarto document (see \code{\link[=maidr_on]{maidr_on()}}). Nothing else sets it:
not the size of the device a chart was drawn on, nor the size of the
window, viewer or Shiny output it is shown in, which shrinks a chart
wider than itself to fit.

Unset, a chart is 7 x 5 in. A candlestick chart is 12 x 6 in, and is
never drawn smaller than that: quantmod's \code{chartSeries()} needs the room
for its title, date range and date labels. A smaller size asked for is
enlarged, with a message naming the size the chart is drawn at. A side
larger than 50 in is refused, as \code{\link[ggplot2:ggsave]{ggplot2::ggsave()}} refuses one: the
size is in inches, not the pixels \code{\link[=maidr_output]{maidr_output()}} takes.

A Base R chart's margins and text take the same room at every size, so
a chart can be too small for them -- R itself stops with "figure margins
too large" at such a size. maidr draws the chart with the margins and
text size its \code{par()} calls set, so it fits where R draws it with them.
Asked for a size too small, maidr stops too, with an error naming the
size, rather than show an empty chart: draw it larger. With no size
asked for, a chart too small for 7 x 5 in, such as a \code{par(mfrow)} grid
of five rows or more, is drawn on the 7 x 7 in page maidr laid every
Base R chart out on before it drew one at its size. Where that is too
small too, it is drawn on the smallest larger page that leaves each of
its plots a sixth of an inch, 12 px, each way, each side grown in whole
inches only as far as it needs: 7 x 9 in for six rows, 12 x 5 in for
twelve columns. A message names the size it is drawn at. The picture of a
Base R chart maidr cannot read is held to its size in the same way.
}

